1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,839
September 11, 1942.

2
00:00:02,839 --> 00:00:07,871
An American submarine is 120 feet under the surface,

3
00:00:07,871 --> 00:00:09,420
in enemy waters.

4
00:00:09,870 --> 00:00:12,670
In the officers' wardroom, the dining table has

5
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:14,219
become an operating table.

6
00:00:14,669 --> 00:00:17,216
The man about to operate is 23 years old.

7
00:00:17,216 --> 00:00:19,702
He is a pharmacist's mate, not a doctor.

8
00:00:19,702 --> 00:00:21,318
There is no doctor aboard.

9
00:00:21,768 --> 00:00:24,891
His instruments include 5 tablespoons, bent back to

10
00:00:24,891 --> 00:00:28,014
hold the incision open, and an ether mask made from

11
00:00:28,014 --> 00:00:28,933
a tea strainer.

12
00:00:29,383 --> 00:00:31,779
The patient is a 19-year-old seaman.

13
00:00:31,779 --> 00:00:35,174
His appendix is inflamed, and part of it is already

14
00:00:35,174 --> 00:00:36,106
turning black.

15
00:00:36,556 --> 00:00:38,065
If they wait, it can burst.

16
00:00:38,065 --> 00:00:40,636
If they operate, the man holding the knife has

17
00:00:40,636 --> 00:00:43,319
helped at a few appendectomies, as an assistant.

18
00:00:43,769 --> 00:00:46,655
This is the true story of that operation, the two

19
00:00:46,655 --> 00:00:49,717
that followed it, and the Navy job it helped create.

20
00:00:50,117 --> 00:00:51,441
No doctor aboard.

21
00:00:53,841 --> 00:00:56,761
The submarine was USS Seadragon.

22
00:00:57,761 --> 00:00:59,637
She was commissioned in 1939.

23
00:00:59,637 --> 00:01:02,353
When the Pacific war began, she was in the

24
00:01:02,353 --> 00:01:05,781
Philippines, at the Navy Yard in Cavite, near Manila.

25
00:01:06,231 --> 00:01:09,977
On December 10, 1941, Japanese bombers struck the yard.

26
00:01:09,977 --> 00:01:13,722
Seadragon was moored beside another submarine, Sealion.

27
00:01:14,172 --> 00:01:16,434
Sealion took a direct hit and was wrecked.

28
00:01:16,434 --> 00:01:19,020
The blast ripped off part of Seadragon's bridge.

29
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,768
Shrapnel pierced her conning tower, killing one man

30
00:01:21,768 --> 00:01:22,738
and wounding five.

31
00:01:23,188 --> 00:01:25,874
One of the men at Cavite that day was a young

32
00:01:25,874 --> 00:01:28,143
pharmacist's mate named Wheeler Lipes.

33
00:01:28,593 --> 00:01:31,564
Lipes was born in 1920, in New Castle, Virginia.

34
00:01:31,564 --> 00:01:34,350
When the bombers came, he was aboard Sealion.

35
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:37,693
He ended up in the water and was picked up that night.

36
00:01:37,693 --> 00:01:40,850
Seadragon's own pharmacist's mate had been wounded,

37
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:42,397
and Lipes took his place.

38
00:01:42,847 --> 00:01:46,159
He had joined the Navy in 1936, trained in its

39
00:01:46,159 --> 00:01:49,688
hospital course in San Diego, and worked in naval

40
00:01:49,688 --> 00:01:52,712
hospitals in Philadelphia and near Manila.

41
00:01:52,712 --> 00:01:54,944
In 1941, he went to submarines.

42
00:01:55,394 --> 00:01:58,060
His specialty was the electrocardiograph, the

43
00:01:58,060 --> 00:01:59,897
machine that records the heart.

44
00:01:59,897 --> 00:02:02,385
He had assisted at several appendectomies.

45
00:02:02,385 --> 00:02:03,629
He was not a surgeon.

46
00:02:04,079 --> 00:02:07,048
When the newspapers later described him, they called

47
00:02:07,048 --> 00:02:09,789
him a lab technician who had worked 3 years in a

48
00:02:09,789 --> 00:02:11,045
Philadelphia hospital.

49
00:02:11,495 --> 00:02:13,743
Seadragon got out of the Philippines.

50
00:02:13,743 --> 00:02:16,660
By the late summer of 1942, she was sailing from

51
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:18,605
Fremantle, in Western Australia.

52
00:02:19,055 --> 00:02:20,672
And then, one of her crew got sick.

53
00:02:21,672 --> 00:02:25,202
On August 26, Seadragon left Australia on her fourth

54
00:02:25,202 --> 00:02:28,460
war patrol, bound for the shipping lanes off the

55
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:29,750
coast of Indochina.

56
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:31,040
55 men were aboard.

57
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:33,401
Seadragon carried no doctor.

58
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:36,201
For medical care, those 55 men had Lipes.

59
00:02:36,651 --> 00:02:39,744
A pharmacist's mate was one of the Navy's enlisted

60
00:02:39,744 --> 00:02:41,229
medical men, a corpsman.

61
00:02:41,229 --> 00:02:44,446
On a submarine, he was the whole medical department.

62
00:02:44,895 --> 00:02:47,749
On September 8, a 19-year-old seaman reported

63
00:02:47,749 --> 00:02:48,574
feeling sick.

64
00:02:48,574 --> 00:02:51,555
Lipes later remembered finding him in the after

65
00:02:51,555 --> 00:02:54,155
battery compartment, the crew's quarters.

66
00:02:54,605 --> 00:02:56,056
His words were simple.

67
00:02:56,056 --> 00:02:58,299
"Hey Doc, I don't feel very good."

68
00:02:58,899 --> 00:03:02,769
By September 11, three days later, the diagnosis was

69
00:03:02,769 --> 00:03:03,737
appendicitis.

70
00:03:04,187 --> 00:03:06,993
The appendix is a small, finger-shaped pouch

71
00:03:06,993 --> 00:03:09,035
attached to the large intestine.

72
00:03:09,035 --> 00:03:12,288
The National Institutes of Health says about 5 to 9

73
00:03:12,288 --> 00:03:15,796
of every 100 people develop appendicitis at some point.

74
00:03:16,246 --> 00:03:18,682
And the NIH is blunt about the danger.

75
00:03:18,682 --> 00:03:21,374
Untreated, an inflamed appendix can burst.

76
00:03:21,374 --> 00:03:24,644
One result is peritonitis, a serious infection that

77
00:03:24,644 --> 00:03:26,631
can spread through the abdomen.

78
00:03:27,081 --> 00:03:30,335
Seadragon was several days and thousands of miles

79
00:03:30,335 --> 00:03:33,124
out from her base at Fremantle, Australia.

80
00:03:33,574 --> 00:03:36,384
What happened next would end up in a Pulitzer

81
00:03:36,384 --> 00:03:39,631
Prize-winning newspaper story, in a Hollywood movie,

82
00:03:39,631 --> 00:03:42,254
and, more than 60 years later, in a medal.

83
00:03:42,654 --> 00:03:44,246
So the captain had to decide.

84
00:03:45,246 --> 00:03:47,769
Seadragon's commanding officer was Lieutenant

85
00:03:47,769 --> 00:03:49,227
Commander William Ferrall.

86
00:03:49,227 --> 00:03:52,423
He asked Lipes whether he could do the operation himself.

87
00:03:52,873 --> 00:03:54,085
Lipes said he could.

88
00:03:54,085 --> 00:03:56,448
And then he told the captain the truth.

89
00:03:57,048 --> 00:03:59,441
In the Navy's account, he went further.

90
00:03:59,441 --> 00:04:01,343
Our chances, he said, are slim.

91
00:04:01,743 --> 00:04:05,261
Decades later, Lipes recalled what the captain said next.

92
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:08,470
"I fire torpedoes every day, and some of them miss."

93
00:04:08,470 --> 00:04:11,494
Lipes answered that this was one torpedo he could

94
00:04:11,494 --> 00:04:12,605
not fire and miss.

95
00:04:13,055 --> 00:04:14,702
The patient had a say too.

96
00:04:14,702 --> 00:04:17,997
His answer, as the Navy's historians record it, was:

97
00:04:17,997 --> 00:04:21,545
"Whatever the doc feels has to be done is okay with me."

98
00:04:21,995 --> 00:04:23,565
Ferrall gave the order.

99
00:04:23,565 --> 00:04:24,929
Lipes would operate.

100
00:04:25,379 --> 00:04:28,462
First, the captain took Seadragon into relatively

101
00:04:28,462 --> 00:04:29,154
safe water.

102
00:04:29,154 --> 00:04:31,860
Then he dived, and leveled off at 120 feet.

103
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:35,625
A hundred and twenty feet of sea above them.

104
00:04:35,625 --> 00:04:36,680
55 men inside.

105
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:40,371
And a 23-year-old pharmacist's mate at the table.

106
00:04:40,821 --> 00:04:44,228
Now he had to build an operating room inside a submarine.

107
00:04:45,228 --> 00:04:48,045
The operating table was the wardroom mess table,

108
00:04:48,045 --> 00:04:49,395
where the officers ate.

109
00:04:49,845 --> 00:04:52,643
His medical kit held a pocket case with 12

110
00:04:52,643 --> 00:04:55,775
hemostats, scalpel blades, catgut for stitches,

111
00:04:55,775 --> 00:04:58,773
sulfa tablets, and a limited supply of ether.

112
00:04:59,223 --> 00:05:01,530
There was no handle for the scalpel blades.

113
00:05:01,530 --> 00:05:03,783
So one of the hemostats became the handle.

114
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:07,125
There were no surgical retractors, the tools that

115
00:05:07,125 --> 00:05:08,187
hold a wound open.

116
00:05:08,187 --> 00:05:11,315
So they took 5 tablespoons and bent the handles back.

117
00:05:11,765 --> 00:05:14,515
For sponges, they used commercially sterilized

118
00:05:14,515 --> 00:05:16,787
Handi-pads, in their tissue envelopes.

119
00:05:17,237 --> 00:05:18,769
There was no anesthesia mask.

120
00:05:18,769 --> 00:05:21,410
So they covered a tea strainer with gauze and gave

121
00:05:21,410 --> 00:05:22,625
the ether through that.

122
00:05:23,075 --> 00:05:24,684
Instruments were boiled.

123
00:05:24,684 --> 00:05:27,768
Gloves and pajamas were sterilized in alcohol.

124
00:05:27,768 --> 00:05:28,841
Torpedo alcohol.

125
00:05:29,291 --> 00:05:32,471
The report even records the air inside the boat:

126
00:05:32,471 --> 00:05:35,254
2 inches of mercury above normal pressure.

127
00:05:35,254 --> 00:05:36,911
The stitches would be No.

128
00:05:36,911 --> 00:05:38,568
0, 20-day chromic catgut.

129
00:05:39,018 --> 00:05:41,832
Lipes ground sulfa tablets into powder, and baked

130
00:05:41,832 --> 00:05:44,760
the powder in the boat's oven to kill anything that

131
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,024
might be living in it.

132
00:05:46,474 --> 00:05:48,871
The anesthetist was not a doctor either.

133
00:05:48,871 --> 00:05:51,448
He was Lieutenant Franz Hoskins, the boat's

134
00:05:51,448 --> 00:05:52,827
communications officer.

135
00:05:53,277 --> 00:05:55,924
And the captain stood by, writing down what he saw

136
00:05:55,924 --> 00:05:57,724
every few minutes, for the record.

137
00:05:58,174 --> 00:06:01,831
Those notes went into a report to the Navy's Bureau

138
00:06:01,831 --> 00:06:05,273
of Medicine and Surgery, dated October 22, 1942.

139
00:06:05,273 --> 00:06:08,787
It is the reason we know the times to the minute.

140
00:06:09,237 --> 00:06:10,518
Then the clock started.

141
00:06:11,518 --> 00:06:13,097
Here is where things stood.

142
00:06:13,097 --> 00:06:15,143
A sick 19-year-old on a mess table.

143
00:06:15,143 --> 00:06:18,184
A pharmacist's mate with a tea strainer, five spoons

144
00:06:18,184 --> 00:06:19,236
and a pocket case.

145
00:06:19,236 --> 00:06:21,692
A communications officer giving the ether.

146
00:06:21,692 --> 00:06:23,213
A captain with a notebook.

147
00:06:23,213 --> 00:06:26,136
And a submarine holding her depth in enemy waters.

148
00:06:26,586 --> 00:06:29,267
At 10:46 in the morning, the ether began.

149
00:06:29,717 --> 00:06:33,343
21 minutes later, at 11:07, Lipes made the first

150
00:06:33,343 --> 00:06:35,231
cut, about 3 inches long.

151
00:06:35,681 --> 00:06:38,609
All around them, the rest of the crew kept the boat

152
00:06:38,609 --> 00:06:39,011
steady.

153
00:06:39,011 --> 00:06:41,710
In the after torpedo room, men waited for news.

154
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:44,742
George Weller, the reporter who later told the

155
00:06:44,742 --> 00:06:47,605
story, wrote that the order on the boat was simple.

156
00:06:47,605 --> 00:06:48,503
Keep her steady.

157
00:06:48,953 --> 00:06:50,605
Finding the appendix took time.

158
00:06:50,605 --> 00:06:53,377
When Lipes found it, the end of it had turned black.

159
00:06:53,377 --> 00:06:54,443
Gangrene had set in.

160
00:06:54,893 --> 00:06:56,946
Years later he put it this way:

161
00:06:56,946 --> 00:07:00,059
it wasn't one of those snappy-valve appendixes.

162
00:07:00,459 --> 00:07:02,513
At 12:30, the appendix came out.

163
00:07:02,513 --> 00:07:05,723
Lipes cauterized the stump with carbolic acid, and

164
00:07:05,723 --> 00:07:08,676
kept the appendix in a jar of torpedo alcohol.

165
00:07:09,126 --> 00:07:11,894
Then he closed the wound layer by layer, dusting

166
00:07:11,894 --> 00:07:14,144
each layer with the baked sulfa powder.

167
00:07:14,594 --> 00:07:17,797
At 1:22 in the afternoon, the last stitch was in.

168
00:07:18,247 --> 00:07:21,606
2 hours and 36 minutes, from the first breath of

169
00:07:21,606 --> 00:07:23,355
ether to the last stitch.

170
00:07:23,355 --> 00:07:25,524
A hundred and twenty feet down.

171
00:07:25,974 --> 00:07:27,754
Now they waited for him to wake up.

172
00:07:28,754 --> 00:07:29,234
He did.

173
00:07:29,234 --> 00:07:32,663
The newspaper story that later made this operation

174
00:07:32,663 --> 00:07:34,857
famous reported his first words.

175
00:07:35,307 --> 00:07:36,754
"I'm still in there pitching."

176
00:07:37,554 --> 00:07:41,223
His temperature climbed the next day, to 103.6, and

177
00:07:41,223 --> 00:07:42,302
then came down.

178
00:07:42,302 --> 00:07:45,036
Within a few days he was back on duty.

179
00:07:45,486 --> 00:07:48,600
When he had trouble after the operation, a period

180
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:51,650
the report calls post-operative depression, they

181
00:07:51,650 --> 00:07:54,319
gave him oxygen, and he improved markedly.

182
00:07:54,319 --> 00:07:57,560
6 hours after he came round, he was drinking water.

183
00:07:58,010 --> 00:08:00,181
The cook had a theory about the recovery.

184
00:08:00,181 --> 00:08:02,775
"Doc," he told Lipes, "you must have sewed him up

185
00:08:02,775 --> 00:08:04,682
with rubber bands, the way he eats."

186
00:08:05,132 --> 00:08:07,681
Seadragon went on with her war patrol.

187
00:08:07,681 --> 00:08:10,833
On October 10, she sank the Japanese cargo ship

188
00:08:10,833 --> 00:08:14,455
Shigitre Maru, 47 seconds after the first torpedo hit.

189
00:08:14,905 --> 00:08:16,033
She kept fighting.

190
00:08:16,033 --> 00:08:19,227
On September 22 she attacked a cruiser, and missed.

191
00:08:19,227 --> 00:08:22,421
On September 30, a surface attack damaged a ship in

192
00:08:22,421 --> 00:08:22,984
a convoy.

193
00:08:23,434 --> 00:08:27,026
She came back to port about six weeks after the operation.

194
00:08:27,026 --> 00:08:30,060
The squadron medical officer examined the patient

195
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:31,917
and found a firm, healed scar.

196
00:08:31,917 --> 00:08:34,951
His conclusion, as the Navy's historians tell it:

197
00:08:34,951 --> 00:08:38,356
Lipes and his shipmates had saved the young man's life.

198
00:08:38,806 --> 00:08:41,968
The Navy's official history of Seadragon gives the

199
00:08:41,968 --> 00:08:43,613
whole drama a single line.

200
00:08:43,613 --> 00:08:46,775
Her progress, it says, was delayed by an emergency

201
00:08:46,775 --> 00:08:49,494
appendectomy, performed successfully by the

202
00:08:49,494 --> 00:08:50,632
pharmacist's mate.

203
00:08:51,082 --> 00:08:54,258
But the official report also carried a warning.

204
00:08:54,258 --> 00:08:57,231
Major surgery by anyone other than qualified

205
00:08:57,231 --> 00:09:01,149
surgeons, it said, was by no means desirable to encourage.

206
00:09:01,599 --> 00:09:04,034
The Navy may not have wanted to encourage it.

207
00:09:04,034 --> 00:09:05,766
America wanted to hear about it.

208
00:09:06,766 --> 00:09:09,845
After the patrol, a war correspondent named George

209
00:09:09,845 --> 00:09:13,110
Weller met Lipes aboard the submarine tender Holland.

210
00:09:13,110 --> 00:09:15,573
Weller wrote for the Chicago Daily News.

211
00:09:16,023 --> 00:09:19,280
Lipes himself was back in the United States by

212
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:20,200
January 1943.

213
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:24,094
By then, his name was in newspapers across the country.

214
00:09:24,544 --> 00:09:27,069
His story ran on December 14, 1942.

215
00:09:27,069 --> 00:09:30,676
He called it the first appendix ever known to have

216
00:09:30,676 --> 00:09:32,984
been removed below enemy waters.

217
00:09:33,434 --> 00:09:36,709
The story won Weller the Pulitzer Prize for

218
00:09:36,709 --> 00:09:38,079
Reporting in 1943.

219
00:09:38,079 --> 00:09:41,963
The Pulitzer citation describes a man who performed

220
00:09:41,963 --> 00:09:46,075
an operation for appendicitis, saving a sailor's life.

221
00:09:46,525 --> 00:09:48,097
Hollywood noticed too.

222
00:09:48,097 --> 00:09:51,599
The submarine film Destination Tokyo, released in

223
00:09:51,599 --> 00:09:55,745
1943 with Cary Grant, borrowed the operation for its plot.

224
00:09:56,195 --> 00:09:57,516
Radio told it next.

225
00:09:57,516 --> 00:10:01,063
In May 1943, the program Cavalcade of America aired

226
00:10:01,063 --> 00:10:04,470
an episode called Pharmacist's Mate, First Class,

227
00:10:04,470 --> 00:10:08,087
and Lipes himself spoke at the end of the broadcast.

228
00:10:08,537 --> 00:10:11,712
15 years later, the television series The Silent

229
00:10:11,712 --> 00:10:14,557
Service told it again, in an episode called

230
00:10:14,557 --> 00:10:15,881
Operation Seadragon.

231
00:10:15,881 --> 00:10:19,321
By then, the operation was a legend of the submarine

232
00:10:19,321 --> 00:10:19,718
force.

233
00:10:20,168 --> 00:10:23,437
And before that year was over, it happened again. Twice.

234
00:10:24,437 --> 00:10:27,881
In December 1942, the submarine Grayback was only a

235
00:10:27,881 --> 00:10:31,122
week out of Australia when one of her torpedomen

236
00:10:31,122 --> 00:10:32,878
needed the same operation.

237
00:10:33,328 --> 00:10:37,141
Grayback had sailed from Australia on December 7,

238
00:10:37,141 --> 00:10:40,409
1942, exactly one year after Pearl Harbor.

239
00:10:40,859 --> 00:10:43,995
Her pharmacist's mate, Harry Roby, operated 100 feet

240
00:10:43,995 --> 00:10:46,949
beneath the surface, in the Bismarck Archipelago.

241
00:10:46,949 --> 00:10:50,266
His patient was back on watch by the end of the patrol.

242
00:10:50,716 --> 00:10:53,361
Then, on Christmas Eve, aboard the submarine

243
00:10:53,361 --> 00:10:56,488
Silversides, the pharmacist's mate performed another

244
00:10:56,488 --> 00:10:57,871
emergency appendectomy.

245
00:10:57,871 --> 00:11:00,877
It was over at 4 in the morning, on Christmas Day.

246
00:11:01,327 --> 00:11:04,396
Silversides already knew what the war could cost.

247
00:11:04,396 --> 00:11:07,653
In May of that year, an enemy machine-gun bullet had

248
00:11:07,653 --> 00:11:09,594
killed one of her deck gunners.

249
00:11:10,044 --> 00:11:11,253
Three submarines.

250
00:11:11,253 --> 00:11:13,031
Three pharmacist's mates.

251
00:11:13,031 --> 00:11:16,657
Three emergency appendectomies in about 3.5 months.

252
00:11:16,657 --> 00:11:19,359
All three were reported as successful.

253
00:11:19,809 --> 00:11:23,282
None of the three men holding the knife was a doctor.

254
00:11:23,282 --> 00:11:26,558
Each one had a crew at sea, far from any hospital,

255
00:11:26,558 --> 00:11:29,768
and a shipmate who needed an emergency operation.

256
00:11:30,218 --> 00:11:32,609
What the Navy learned from those months is still

257
00:11:32,609 --> 00:11:33,306
sailing today.

258
00:11:34,306 --> 00:11:37,433
In 1944, the Navy identified a need for medical

259
00:11:37,433 --> 00:11:40,692
personnel who could work where no medical officer

260
00:11:40,692 --> 00:11:41,623
was available.

261
00:11:41,623 --> 00:11:44,151
They became Independent Duty Corpsmen.

262
00:11:44,601 --> 00:11:48,164
Their first course, an intermediate course at the

263
00:11:48,164 --> 00:11:51,800
Hospital Corps School in Portsmouth, Virginia, was

264
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:54,927
245 hours in the classroom and 235 hours of

265
00:11:54,927 --> 00:11:56,381
practical exercises.

266
00:11:56,381 --> 00:12:00,090
Submarine corpsmen trained in a separate course, in

267
00:12:00,090 --> 00:12:01,544
Groton, Connecticut.

268
00:12:01,994 --> 00:12:05,777
In 1949, that course gave way to a 20-week advanced

269
00:12:05,777 --> 00:12:07,260
course in San Diego.

270
00:12:07,260 --> 00:12:09,782
A year later, it grew to 26 weeks.

271
00:12:09,782 --> 00:12:13,639
As of 2014, independent duty corpsman courses ran in

272
00:12:13,639 --> 00:12:17,125
North Carolina, Connecticut and California, and

273
00:12:17,125 --> 00:12:20,536
submarine service also required Basic Enlisted

274
00:12:20,536 --> 00:12:21,797
Submarine School.

275
00:12:22,247 --> 00:12:25,773
Corpsmen have served aboard American submarines

276
00:12:25,773 --> 00:12:26,598
since 1924.

277
00:12:27,048 --> 00:12:30,598
From 1959 into the 1970s, the big missile submarines

278
00:12:30,598 --> 00:12:32,032
did carry physicians.

279
00:12:32,032 --> 00:12:35,583
Each of their two crews, Blue and Gold, had a doctor

280
00:12:35,583 --> 00:12:37,836
and an independent duty corpsman.

281
00:12:37,836 --> 00:12:41,250
Even then, according to a Navy medicine historian,

282
00:12:41,250 --> 00:12:44,801
the corpsmen handled most of the daily medical care.

283
00:12:45,251 --> 00:12:48,328
One of those submarine doctors later told a Navy

284
00:12:48,328 --> 00:12:51,341
historian about setting a broken leg at sea and

285
00:12:51,341 --> 00:12:53,200
putting it in a plaster cast.

286
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:56,342
The submarine stayed on patrol, with about 2 or 3

287
00:12:56,342 --> 00:12:57,495
weeks still to go.

288
00:12:57,945 --> 00:12:59,106
That did not last.

289
00:12:59,106 --> 00:13:02,329
A study sponsored by the Navy's Bureau of Medicine

290
00:13:02,329 --> 00:13:05,422
later found that independent duty corpsmen could

291
00:13:05,422 --> 00:13:07,936
handle most submarine medical services.

292
00:13:08,386 --> 00:13:11,605
Navy medicine calls the job a department of one.

293
00:13:11,605 --> 00:13:14,957
As one independent duty corpsman, the only medical

294
00:13:14,957 --> 00:13:17,773
provider on his ship, told Navy reporters:

295
00:13:18,223 --> 00:13:20,697
His work, as Navy medicine described it:

296
00:13:20,697 --> 00:13:23,604
sick call, medical training for the crew, daily

297
00:13:23,604 --> 00:13:26,573
water testing, and pest surveys every two weeks.

298
00:13:26,573 --> 00:13:29,665
He reported directly to the commanding officer and

299
00:13:29,665 --> 00:13:32,881
the executive officer, and he looked after a crew of

300
00:13:32,881 --> 00:13:34,118
more than a hundred.

301
00:13:34,568 --> 00:13:37,170
"The work is stressful because all of the

302
00:13:37,170 --> 00:13:40,280
responsibility and accountability lies completely

303
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:40,851
with me."

304
00:13:41,451 --> 00:13:45,589
That is the job Wheeler Lipes did at 23, 120 feet

305
00:13:45,589 --> 00:13:48,291
down, before it even had a name.

306
00:13:48,741 --> 00:13:50,165
And the people in this story?

307
00:13:51,165 --> 00:13:53,652
Seadragon served through the whole war.

308
00:13:53,652 --> 00:13:56,905
She made 12 war patrols and earned 11 battle stars.

309
00:13:56,905 --> 00:14:00,222
Among the ships she is credited with sinking was the

310
00:14:00,222 --> 00:14:01,689
Japanese submarine I-4.

311
00:14:02,139 --> 00:14:04,411
Grayback did not come home.

312
00:14:04,411 --> 00:14:09,291
She was listed as overdue and presumed lost in March 1944.

313
00:14:09,741 --> 00:14:13,566
The young seaman Lipes saved did not survive the war

314
00:14:13,566 --> 00:14:14,080
either.

315
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:17,905
In October 1944, he was serving aboard the submarine

316
00:14:17,905 --> 00:14:19,597
Tang when she was lost.

317
00:14:19,597 --> 00:14:21,656
Only 9 of her crew survived.

318
00:14:22,106 --> 00:14:23,928
Lipes stayed in the Navy.

319
00:14:23,928 --> 00:14:27,646
He was commissioned in the Medical Service Corps in

320
00:14:27,646 --> 00:14:31,436
1951, served until 1962, and retired as a lieutenant

321
00:14:31,436 --> 00:14:32,165
commander.

322
00:14:32,165 --> 00:14:33,769
Then he ran hospitals.

323
00:14:33,769 --> 00:14:37,413
When he retired for good in 1991, he was president

324
00:14:37,413 --> 00:14:41,203
of Memorial Medical Center in Corpus Christi, Texas.

325
00:14:41,653 --> 00:14:43,638
Wheeler Lipes lived to be 84.

326
00:14:43,638 --> 00:14:46,787
In February 2005, more than 60 years after the

327
00:14:46,787 --> 00:14:50,004
operation, the Navy presented him with the Navy

328
00:14:50,004 --> 00:14:53,221
Commendation Medal for saving a crewman's life.

329
00:14:53,671 --> 00:14:56,585
The ceremony was at Camp Lejeune, North Carolina.

330
00:14:56,585 --> 00:14:59,381
Years earlier, Lipes recalled, his grandson had

331
00:14:59,381 --> 00:15:02,236
found the story in a schoolbook, and his teacher

332
00:15:02,236 --> 00:15:03,961
told him not to tell stories.

333
00:15:04,411 --> 00:15:05,732
He died that April.

334
00:15:06,332 --> 00:15:07,630
Twenty-3 years old.

335
00:15:07,630 --> 00:15:09,748
A hundred and twenty feet down.

336
00:15:09,748 --> 00:15:13,506
Five bent spoons, a tea strainer, and no doctor aboard.

337
00:15:13,506 --> 00:15:16,854
And a sailor who woke up still in there pitching.

338
00:15:17,454 --> 00:15:18,919
Next time on KEPT ALIVE:

339
00:15:18,919 --> 00:15:22,459
another life on a clock, and the people who kept it going.

340
00:15:22,909 --> 00:15:24,050
For education only.

341
00:15:24,050 --> 00:15:25,671
This is not medical advice.

342
00:15:25,671 --> 00:15:28,492
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
